1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,024
Where does a payment system start, if not with payments?

2
00:00:03,074 --> 00:00:07,560
With a stack, a build, and an environment that runs with one command.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:14,976
We chose Java twenty-five and Spring Boot four: long-term support, and the most widely used stack for Java services.

4
00:00:15,026 --> 00:00:27,384
We chose Gradle, with builds in Groovy, because the build is the pipeline: it generates code from the contracts, checks the style, runs the tests, and publishes, the same way on every machine.

5
00:00:27,434 --> 00:00:38,160
The app is one modular monolith with six modules, partner, commission, e POS, BIN, card, and payment: one deploy today, and clean seams for tomorrow.

6
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:46,128
Each module owns its own schema in one database, and connects as its own user, which can see that schema and nothing else.

7
00:00:46,178 --> 00:00:53,304
A module talks to another only through the public interface in its root package, and the build fails if it reaches any further.

8
00:00:53,354 --> 00:01:03,408
Inside, every module uses the one layout every Spring developer knows: layered, with a controller, a service, a repository, and an entity.

9
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:15,792
We chose contract first: an OpenAPI file comes before any code, Gradle generates the interfaces and models, and the code must implement them, so it never drifts from what clients were promised.

10
00:01:15,842 --> 00:01:22,800
The same contract generates a client too, so a caller in another service uses exactly the same interface and models.

11
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:32,160
Shared pieces, the error format, security, persistence, crypto, and every contract, live in the platform folder, each with its own build.

12
00:01:32,210 --> 00:01:43,488
Gradle publishes each one to a Maven repository, and the app imports it by version, like any other library; the day the platform moves to its own repository, nothing else changes.

13
00:01:43,538 --> 00:01:50,544
A version catalog, published the same way, pins every version, so every build uses the same ones.

14
00:01:50,594 --> 00:02:00,648
Errors follow one format: each module lists its errors once, and the client gets a problem detail with a stable code, in English, Russian or Uzbek.

15
00:02:00,698 --> 00:02:09,336
Services that do one job live outside the app, each with its own contract and client: the card scheme adapters, and the file service.

16
00:02:09,386 --> 00:02:25,488
A separate infrastructure repository starts the whole infrastructure with Docker Compose: Postgres and Redis, Mailpit, an S3 store, Keycloak, a processing sandbox, Vault, monitoring, Temporal and Kafka, and each one is explained in the episode that first uses it.

17
00:02:25,538 --> 00:02:33,864
One command, and the whole world is up; the code, the infrastructure, the load tests, and the web app each live in their own repository.

18
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:37,392
Next, the domain, which comes before a single table.
